roots-gravity-ask = A jar of seedlings is laid on its side. Which way will the roots turn?
roots-gravity-title = The root knows which way is down
roots-gravity-question = Maize grains have sprouted in a glass jar behind a wet paper towel, the roots growing straight down. We lay the jar on its side — the roots lie level. What becomes of them in two hours?
roots-gravity-choices = They bend down
roots-gravity-choices-2 = They go on growing sideways, as they lie
roots-gravity-choices-3 = They turn up, to the light
roots-gravity-asides = And in 1806 the Englishman Thomas Andrew Knight fooled the seedlings. He tied sprouted beans to the rim of a wheel turned by water — over 150 turns a minute. The roots turned outwards from the axle, and in a few days the shoots met tip to tip in the centre of the wheel: for the plants, the spinning stood in for gravity.
roots-gravity-asides-2 = In 1880 Charles Darwin with his son Francis published «The Power of Movement in Plants». They found that it is the very tip of the root that feels gravity, and wrote that the tip of the radicle «acts like the brain of one of the lower animals».
roots-gravity-asides-3 = And what becomes of roots where there is no down at all? In 2012 scientists grew thale cress on the International Space Station and photographed it every 6 hours. Even weightless, the roots did not grow anyhow: light set their direction — they grew away from it.
roots-gravity-answer-why =
    A root grows towards the Earth and a stem away from it: that is how gravity acts on plants. And it is the root tip that feels it. Its cells hold grains of starch, heavier than the rest of the cell’s contents. They settle to the bottom, like sand in a jar of water. When the root is laid on its side, the grains slide to the new lower wall. Then the root’s upper side grows faster than its lower side — and the root bends down.

    On the clinostat the jar keeps turning, and «down» goes round for the grains. They never have time to settle anywhere — as if weightless, so the root does not bend. The clinostat was invented by the German botanist Julius Sachs in 1879.
